\transpose transposes a stored bar by a defined number
of semitones.
This command can only be used inside a bar definition.
Its syntax is the following:

\transpose {\barname numsemitones}

where "\barname" is the name of a stored bar and
"numseminotes" is a number that defines how many
semitones the bar should be transposed.
For example, if we define the following bar:

\bar transp-1 {
    \inst1 c'2\f cis'4. c'16 d'
    \inst2 g2\f gis2
}

then we can use this command to transpose the notes
like in the example below:

\bar transp-2 {
    \inst1 \transpose {\transp-1 2}
    \inst2 \transpose {\transp-1 2}
}

We can also use this command to retrieve the notes
from another bar and another instrument, like the
example below:

\bar transp-3 {
    \inst1 \transpose {\transp-2.\inst2 10}
    \inst2 \transpose {\transp-2.\inst1 -14}
}

The example above will result in a bar with the notes of
bar \transp-1, where each instrument plays the notes of
the other instrument, with \inst1 playing the notes of \inst2
transposed one octave above, and \inst2 playting the notes
of \inst1 transposed one octave below.
